    58	- [scx_mitosis](scx_mitosis/README.md)
    59	- [scx_pandemonium](scx_pandemonium/README.md)
    60	- [scx_p2dq](scx_p2dq/README.md)
    61	- [scx_rustland](scx_rustland/README.md)
    62	- [scx_rusty](scx_rusty/README.md)
    63	- [scx_tickless](scx_tickless/README.md)
    64	SCHED_EXT SCHEDULERS
    65	====================
    66
    67	# Introduction
    68
    69	This directory contains the repo's schedulers.
    70
    71	Some of these schedulers are simply examples of different types of schedulers
    72	that can be built using `sched_ext`. They can be loaded and used to schedule on
    73	your system, but their primary purpose is to illustrate how various features of
    74	`sched_ext` can be used.
    75
    76	Other schedulers are actually performant, production-ready schedulers. That is,
    77	for the correct workload and with the correct tuning, they may be deployed in a
    78	production environment with acceptable or possibly even improved performance.
    79	Some of the examples could be improved to become production schedulers.
    80
    81	Please see the following `README` files for details on each of the various types
    82	of schedulers:
    83
    84	- [`rust`](rust/README.md) describes all of the schedulers with `Rust`
    85	  user space components.
    86
    87
    88	# Note on C schedulers
    89
    90	This directory previously also held C schedulers for illustration purposes. These
    91	schedulers are now found only in the Linux kernel repository in
    92	[`tools/sched_ext`](https://git.kernel.org/pub/scm/linux/kernel/git/torvalds/linux.git/tree/tools/sched_ext).
    93	Each C scheduler's purpose was to demonstrate a single technique for development,
    94	often BPF-related. The C schedulers were kept synced between upstream and this
    95	repository.
    96
    97	As the ecosystem has matured these schedulers have been superseded by those in the
    98	`rust/` directory, many of which are production-ready. These schedulers better represent
    99	modern `sched_ext` codebases and are a more appropriate starting point for newcomers.
   100	The build system and surrouding crates ecosystem is also geared towards Rust schedulers.
   101	As a result, the C schedulers are no longer mirrored here. They are available
   102	from the kernel/tools/sched_ext, and also in the dedicated
   103	[scx-c-examples](https://github.com/sched-ext/scx-c-examples) repository for
   104	those who want to explore or experiment with C-based schedulers.

